@@ -0,0 +1,33 @@
+# Scientific adapter and controlled service
+
+This release implements execution plumbing. It does not qualify the native PSeq workflow, approve data/references/QC, install a service or authorize a pilot. The shipped catalog contains no scientific profile. Keep hosted DPS intentionally stopped until a preserving activation is authorized.
+
+## Configuration
+
+`DPS_RECIPE_CATALOG_PATH` is the single protected catalog. Remove the former `DPS_RECIPE_KEY`, `DPS_RECIPE_VERSION`, `DPS_RECIPE_NAME`, `DPS_RECIPE_PARAMETERS_SCHEMA_JSON`, `DPS_REQUIRED_OUTPUT_ROLES` and `DPS_SUPPORTED_READ_LAYOUTS` from the future runtime configuration conversion; they are no longer read. Retain existing broker/TLS/AWS identity and ledger. `DPS_WORKSPACE_PATH` defaults to `attempts/` beside that ledger. One worker holds the exclusive ledger lock and runs one attempt at a time.
+
+Each `scientific_profiles` entry uses the fields in `ScientificProfile`. An approved profile pins an absolute, read-only executable (`engine`), every transitive tool and every reference file by `name`, actual release `version`, `path`, and lowercase compressed/file-byte `sha256`. Pin a read-only `qc_criteria` document too. Reference database shards must each be listed. Native dependencies and architecture still require actual qualification; a configured version string alone is not version proof. Preserve the native build/version receipts with these pins.
+
+Specify exact frozen `parameters`, supported read layouts, named `stages` with an argument array, and distinct required `outputs` (`role`, relative native-workspace `path`, `format`). Scientific outputs currently support FASTA, JSON, plain text and rectangular TSV/unquoted CSV. Qualify a different parser before enabling quoted CSV or another format. `provenance_report` and `provenance-report.json` are adapter-owned. The adapter appends that required role to Describe. No scientific commands, default SMIDs, sample IDs, references or acceptance values are invented by this release.
+
+The approved executable must consume the generated `bindings.json` and `parameters.json`, directly or through a qualified native adapter. Argument tokens are `{bindings}`, `{parameters}`, `{workspace}` only; arguments never pass through a shell and user values are never interpolated into commands. The same paths are exposed as `DPS_BINDINGS_PATH` and `DPS_PARAMETERS_PATH`. Bindings retain original input IDs/names, exact S3 versions, read/group/part order, local verified file locations, expanded sizes and read-identifier hashes, plus pinned tools/references. Gzip files remain exact compressed originals; decompression is validated and bounded. The engine must process the complete mapping. Any derived concatenation/decompression must be produced inside its workspace and its relationship included in qualified scientific outputs. No directory search or global demonstration dataset is used.
+
+`enabled` defaults false. A nonempty `approval` must bind `profile_sha256`, `dataset_sha256`, `references_sha256`, and `qc_criteria_sha256`. `dataset`, `references`, and `scientific_qc` each require a real approving actor, UTC approval time and retained evidence ID. The protected deployment process records those actual decisions; the digest helper never creates approvals. Dataset binding includes the full specimen/run scope, recipe parameters, ordered file mappings and exact pinned objects, while excluding attempt/time/output prefix so an authorized linked repeat can reuse the same approved dataset. Changing parameters, tool/stage/output/limit/reference/QC pins invalidates the profile approval.
+
+`dps --qualification-digests <catalog> <input-manifest>` prints binding digests without connecting to MQTT, creating a ledger, enabling a recipe or executing native work. `dps --check` validates configuration and prints availability/hold reasons without a broker connection or Start. Unknown command-line options fail without starting the worker.
+
+## Ownership and recovery
+
+Every scientific attempt has a separate versioned `execution.json` sidecar. The existing ledger retains receipts, commands, lifecycle events and final acknowledgment identities in its existing format. Writes flush file and Linux directory metadata before native intent, checkpoint and publication promotion. Never delete/recreate a ledger or attempt directory to make a retry work.
+
+A stage host starts a Linux session, records PID, kernel start ticks, boot identity and nonce, and clears cloud/broker credentials from the native environment. Bounded protected diagnostics are drained even after their retention limit. Cancellation waits for process-group fencing before terminal reporting. The service uses `KillMode=control-group` so worker death also stops owned descendants. A journal with an in-flight stage, missing ownership/result or missing journal for an already-running scientific attempt holds the entire worker for Operations review. It never silently starts that stage again. Keep all such evidence, stop/fence the exact owned processes, and resolve the observation through a separately reviewed reconciliation; do not hand-edit the journal or fabricate a stop time. Automatic computation recovery from an ambiguous stage is deliberately unavailable.
+
+Completed stages are not repeated during output recovery. Outputs are frozen into publication copies, validated, assigned durable IDs and written create-only inside the authorized attempt prefix. S3 version locators are journaled before verification. A retry with a recorded version re-verifies it; an upload intent without a durable locator is held rather than overwritten/reuploaded. A published manifest reuses its exact bytes, hash, versions and stop time. Final ledger events/ACKs remain idempotent. 100 percent belongs to verified manifest completion, never a native process exit. A temporary Portal admission/scan failure preserves DPS Succeeded and retries file/analysis admission without another Start.
+
+## Release preparation and remaining gates
+
+`prepare-release.sh` is a preparation-only Linux ARM64 build procedure using exact committed source, the existing SDK and locked Cargo dependencies. It does not install missing tools. The native `phaeno` candidate and transitive tool/reference releases, complete CLI/sample mapping and output roles must be qualified against the approved dataset; these build assets have not done that qualification. Store an actual native version readback alongside hashes, compiler/toolchain identities and measured resource budgets before activation.
+
+Review the supplied service paths against the protected runtime configuration and preserve the existing user, certificates, ledger and backups. Pin scientific binaries/reference stores outside the writable attempt root; the service makes the rest of the filesystem read-only. Prepare configuration conversion and backup/restore evidence before any activation. `Restart=on-failure` is bounded to three starts per five minutes; an intentional service stop stays stopped. The stop hook records systemd exit code, signal/status, result and build identity in the journal. Do not install, enable or start this unit as a side effect of preparing it.
+
+Initial scientific output files are capped at 100 MiB and the set at 400,000,000 bytes. Portal additionally checks its effective scanner/file/set limits including parameters before admission. Expanded input, workspace, free-space reserve, run-duration and diagnostic budgets are profile-bound. The parent samples workspace growth/free disk each second and fences processing on a limit breach; allocation can exceed the soft sampled limit between samples, so a qualified host/filesystem quota is still needed for a hard scratch bound. The supplied service also caps memory at 50 GiB (48 GiB high watermark) and 512 tasks. Measure and review these limits against the approved dataset before activation. Do not disable scanning, increase limits or downsample data silently. Provider success is separate from independent scientific QC and Customer release.
